    {
        "title": "The Teen Sleep Crisis Is a Schedule Problem, Not a Character Problem",
        "slug": "teen-sleep-crisis", "section": "family-pregnancy",
        "subcategory": "Parenting",
        "summary": "Adolescent body clocks genuinely shift later — then first period starts at 8 a.m. anyway. Here's what families can and can't control.",
        "body_html": "<p>At puberty, the circadian clock shifts about two hours later: melatonin release moves toward 11 p.m., making a 10 p.m. bedtime biologically unavailable to many teens no matter how responsible they are. Combine that with early school start times and the arithmetic fails — which is how 7 in 10 high schoolers end up below the recommended 8 to 10 hours. Chronic teen sleep loss tracks with worse mood, anxiety, grades, driving safety, and sports injury rates.</p><p>Families control the margins, and the margins matter: a consistent wake time (weekend sleep-ins capped at an hour or two, since three-hour lie-ins jet-lag the clock by Monday), phones charging outside the bedroom overnight — the single highest-yield rule, both for light exposure and 1 a.m. social pull — caffeine ended by early afternoon, and morning bright light, which nudges the clock earlier. Framing matters too: negotiate the system with the teen rather than imposing it; sleep-as-performance-enhancer lands better than sleep-as-obedience.</p><p>Some of it is policy, not parenting: the pediatric and sleep-medicine establishment recommends middle and high schools start no earlier than 8:30 a.m., and districts that shifted saw attendance, grades, and car crash rates improve. Meanwhile, if a teen sleeps adequately on their own schedule but simply can't shift earlier, that's delayed sleep phase — common, real, and treatable with timed light and melatonin under guidance rather than escalating bedtime battles.</p>",
        "author": "hannah-bell-curtis", "reviewer": "grace-okonkwo-md",
        "published": "2024-09-02", "reviewed": "2024-09-06",
    },
    {
        "title": "Ear Infections: Why Your Pediatrician Might Not Prescribe Antibiotics Today",
        "slug": "ear-infections-watchful-waiting", "section": "family-pregnancy",
        "subcategory": "Children's Health",
        "summary": "'Watchful waiting' isn't neglect — it's the guideline. Most middle-ear infections clear on their own, and the exceptions are well defined.",
        "body_html": "<p>Acute otitis media — fluid plus inflammation behind the eardrum — is the most common reason children receive antibiotics, and also a leading site of antibiotic overuse: most cases are viral or resolve without treatment within two to three days. Modern guidelines therefore split the decision by age and severity: infants under 6 months and any child who is severely ill get antibiotics promptly, while healthy children over 2 with mild symptoms — and many between 6 and 24 months with one-sided, non-severe infection — do well with 48 to 72 hours of observation and pain control first.</p><p>The practical version of watchful waiting is comfort plus a safety net: weight-dosed acetaminophen or ibuprofen (the ear pain, not the infection, is what torments everyone at 3 a.m.), fluids, and a plan — often a 'rescue prescription' for amoxicillin to fill only if the child isn't improving in two days or worsens. When antibiotics are used, amoxicillin remains first-line, with the full course completed even after the child perks up.</p><p>Prevention is underrated: pneumococcal and flu vaccines cut ear infection rates, breastfeeding is protective in the first year, and eliminating secondhand smoke exposure measurably lowers recurrences, as does ditching the bedtime bottle in bed. For frequent flyers — three infections in six months or four in a year — an ENT conversation about ear tubes is reasonable rather than radical.</p>",
        "author": "hannah-bell-curtis", "reviewer": "danielle-hart-md",
        "published": "2024-10-28", "reviewed": "2024-11-01",
    },
    {
        "title": "Prostate Screening After 50: Making the PSA Decision Like an Adult",
        "slug": "prostate-psa-decision", "section": "family-pregnancy",
        "subcategory": "Men's Health",
        "summary": "The PSA test can save lives and can start cascades of unnecessary treatment. That's why the guideline is a conversation, not a checkbox.",
        "body_html": "<p>Prostate cancer is common and usually slow: most men diagnosed will die with it, not of it — but it remains the second-leading cancer killer of American men, and that tension is the whole PSA debate. The blood test detects trouble early but can't distinguish tigers from housecats, so screening finds many cancers that never needed finding, alongside some that urgently did. Current guidance for men 55 to 69 is shared decision-making: understand the trade-offs, weigh your values, decide deliberately. Higher-risk men — Black men and those with affected fathers or brothers — reasonably start that conversation in their 40s.</p><p>The trade-offs, honestly stated: over roughly a decade, screening prevents a small number of prostate cancer deaths per thousand men screened, at the cost of false alarms, biopsies (with infection risk), and overdiagnosis — treatment of cancers that would have sat harmlessly, with real rates of urinary and sexual side effects. Two modern developments soften the ledger: MRI before biopsy reduces unnecessary sampling, and active surveillance — monitoring low-risk cancers instead of treating them — has become the standard offer, dramatically cutting overtreatment.</p><p>A decision framework that works: if your life expectancy exceeds 10 to 15 years and you'd want to act on an early cancer, screening every 1 to 2 years is a defensible choice; if you'd decline treatment regardless, or the anxiety of surveillance would outweigh the reassurance, declining the test is equally defensible. What's not defensible is drift — reaching 60 without ever having had the conversation. Put it on the agenda at your next physical, alongside the unglamorous checks (blood pressure, lipids, colon screening) that more reliably determine how long you'll live.</p>",
        "author": "curtis-boone", "reviewer": "karen-shale-md",
        "published": "2024-06-10", "reviewed": "2024-06-14",
    },
    {
        "title": "Menopause and Hormone Therapy: The Pendulum Settles",
        "slug": "menopause-hormone-therapy", "section": "family-pregnancy",
        "subcategory": "Women's Health",
        "summary": "After two decades of whiplash, the evidence has landed somewhere reasonable: for the right woman at the right time, hormone therapy is back on the table.",
        "body_html": "<p>The 2002 Women's Health Initiative headlines drove hormone therapy use off a cliff, and a generation of women white-knuckled hot flashes as a result. The subsequent 20 years of reanalysis produced a more useful picture: risk depends heavily on age and timing. For healthy women under 60 and within 10 years of menopause, hormone therapy is the most effective treatment for hot flashes and night sweats, treats genitourinary symptoms, protects bone — and carries absolute risks far smaller than the original headlines implied. Started later, the calculus worsens; that nuance was the story all along.</p><p>The decision still individualizes: personal history of breast cancer, blood clots, stroke, or heart disease pushes against systemic hormones, while for vaginal and urinary symptoms alone, low-dose vaginal estrogen works with minimal systemic absorption and a much shorter warning label. Women with a uterus need progestogen alongside estrogen; formulations and routes (transdermal appears to carry lower clot risk than oral) are legitimately choosable variables, not marketing noise.</p><p>For women who can't or prefer not to use hormones, the toolkit has grown: certain antidepressants (including low-dose SSRIs and venlafaxine) meaningfully reduce hot flashes, a newer non-hormonal drug class targets the brain's temperature circuitry directly, and CBT approaches improve how much the symptoms disrupt life. The unifying advice: menopause care is a legitimate medical visit, not something to endure quietly — and any clinician who waves it off is replaceable.</p>",
        "author": "hannah-bell-curtis", "reviewer": "danielle-hart-md",
        "published": "2024-08-19", "reviewed": "2024-08-23",
    },
    {
        "title": "The Family Medicine Cabinet: What to Stock, What to Toss",
        "slug": "family-medicine-cabinet", "section": "family-pregnancy",
        "subcategory": "Parenting",
        "summary": "Ten items cover most household medical events. The audit takes twenty minutes, ideally before the next 11 p.m. fever.",
        "body_html": "<p>Start with the toss: anything expired (potency and, rarely, safety degrade), anything unlabeled, leftover antibiotics (partial courses are how resistance and wrong-drug errors happen), and — per pediatric guidance — cough-and-cold combination products for children under 4. Dispose properly: drug take-back sites beat the toilet, and the FDA's short 'flush list' (mainly opioids) is the exception. And relocate the whole stash if it lives in the bathroom: humidity is the enemy, and 'up and away' storage matters wherever children visit — grandparents' pill organizers are a leading source of pediatric poisonings.</p><p>The stock list: acetaminophen and ibuprofen in strengths for each family member (children's dosing by weight — keep the syringe, ditch kitchen spoons), an antihistamine (cetirizine or loratadine for daytime allergies; diphenhydramine for allergic reactions, noting its sedation), oral rehydration solution for stomach bugs, a thermometer that works, bandages and antibiotic ointment, hydrocortisone cream, saline spray, and any family-specific rescue items — an epinephrine auto-injector or albuterol inhaler, unexpired and findable in the dark. Post the poison control number (1-800-222-1222) inside the cabinet door.</p><p>Two habits complete the system: a yearly expiration sweep (attach it to daylight saving time, like smoke-alarm batteries), and a one-page medication list per family member — including doses and allergies — photographed on a parent's phone. In an actual emergency, that photo answers the questions the ER asks first, at the moment your memory is least available.</p>",
        "author": "david-lindqvist", "reviewer": "danielle-hart-md",
        "published": "2024-04-15", "reviewed": "2024-04-19",
    },
